---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import TidyTree from '../components/tree/TidyTree.astro';
import DownloadImage from '../components/ui/DownloadImage.astro';
import Figure from '../components/ui/Figure.astro';

import treeData from '../data/program-era/iowa-tree.json';
import Dinkus from '../components/ui/Dinkus.astro';

const pageTitle = "Tidy Tree";
const description = "A radial tidy tree visualization of advisor-student relationships in the Iowa Writers' Workshop poetry program.";
---

<BaseLayout pageTitle={pageTitle} description={description}>

  <p class="title-page">A radial tidy tree visualization of advisor-student relationships in the Iowa Writers' Workshop poetry program.</p>
  <h4 class="title-page">MADE WITH</h4>
  <h3 class="title-page"><a href="https://d3js.org/d3-hierarchy/tree" target="_blank">D3.JS</a> AND <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/news/claude-opus-4-5" target="_blank">OPUS 4.5</a></h3>
  <h4 class="title-page">BASED ON THE TIDY TREE VISUALIZATION ON <a href="https://observablehq.com/@d3/tree-component" target="_blank">OBSERVABLE</a></h4>

  <Dinkus count={3}></Dinkus>


  <Figure>
    <TidyTree slot="image"
    id="iowa-radial-tree"
    treeData={treeData}
    width={778}
    height={550}
    radius={450}
    separation={1.2}
    translateX={90}
    rootNodeRadius={10}
    rootFontSize={15}
    rootFontColor="#5B21B6"
    rootNodeColor="#7C3AED"
    advisorNodeRange={[3, 10]}
    advisorFontRange={[3, 13]}
    advirosFontColor="#6D28D9"
    advisorNodeColor="#8B5CF6"
    advisorLabelDistance={10}
    studentNodeRadius={1.5}
    studentFontSize={3}
    studentFontColor="#EA580C"
    studentNodeColor="#FDBA74"
    studentLabelDistance={10}
    linkColor="#D1D5DB"
    linkStrokeWidth={0.3}
    hoverLinkStrokeWidth={1}
    hoverColor="#EC4899"
    hoverRadiusMultiplier={1.05}
    hoverArcMultiplier={1.5}
    />
    <span slot="caption">
      Figure 1: Multi-generational advisor-student lineages in the Iowa Writers' Workshop poetry program. Click on an advisor (purple node) to display associated students (orange nodes). Data Source: <a href="https://doi.org/10.18737/CNJV1733p4520210415" target="_blank">The Program Era Project</a>.
    </span>
  </Figure>
  <DownloadImage 
    targetId="iowa-radial-tree" 
    filename="the-program-era-tree"
    format="all"
    pngScale={6}
  />

  <Dinkus count={1}></Dinkus>
  <h3>Over Before It Began</h3>
  <p>Dec. 1, 2025</p>
  <!-- <p>Notable lineages include <strong>Paul Engle → Donald Justice → Jorie Graham → Mark Levine</strong> spanning four generations.</p> -->
  <p>I was creating infographics on poetry cultures using Tableau when I remembered why I don’t rely on it much for my research. It’s excellent for statistical visualizations, and you can use it to build some of the prettiest dashboards. When it comes to designing visualizations outside Tableau’s templates or dealing with hundreds or thousands of rows, however, it just feels wonky.</p>
  <p>I enjoy working with the messiness of datasets, and I want my audience to share that experience. Since the field of analytics and visualization is still new to most of us, a visualization that lays out all the data points helps illustrate the process that led to the insights. In other words, visualizing a dataset isn’t just about showing your work—it’s also a fun way to teach others how to read these visualizations.</p>
  <p>As I was working on <i>the Program Era</i> dataset, housed in the wonderful <i>Post45 Data Collective</i>, I couldn’t get the layout I wanted using Tableau’s extensions, and Tableau couldn’t display all the rows either. After a couple of hours of hair-pulling, I gave up on Tableau and went back to making custom visualizations using good old D3.</p>
  <p>Thanks to Claude’s latest model, Opus 4.5, creating the tree diagram above was effortless. It not only generated the python script to restructure the dataset into a nested JSON—the format needed for tree diagrams—but also produced impeccable D3 code. After six hours of vibe coding and burning through my bonus credits on <a href="https://kilo.ai/docs/getting-started/setting-up" target="_blank">Kilo Code</a>, I finally had the diagram I wanted. The next day, I used Sonnet 4.5 to refine the D3 interactions and finalized the visualization shown above. My time with Opus 4.5 was short-lived, but I was quite impressed with its coding capabilities.</p>
  <Dinkus count={3}></Dinkus>

</BaseLayout>
